<!--
    ServiceNow Update Set record-definitions for the TERMINAL-STATE
    read-only rendering of the `x_casemgmt_case` custom table inside the
    scoped application `x_casemgmt`.

    This file produces ELEVEN records - one `sys_ui_policy` row and its
    ten `sys_ui_policy_action` children - which together make the case
    form present a Closed case as the terminal record AAP Section 0.5.5
    says it is, instead of presenting it as an editable record whose
    edits are then refused by the server. It is the `ui_policy/` entry of
    AAP Section 0.4.1 for the case table, alongside the sibling
    x_casemgmt_case_party_conditional_fields.xml which does the same job
    for the party table's polymorphism.

    THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES (QA finding "Issue 17", Functional / Visual)

      Observed on the running application, on the Closed demo cases
      CASE9000006 (General Inquiry) and CASE9000010 (Complaint):

        "Closed records still expose editable fields and Update, relying
         on the server guard to reject the save."

      Every one of the case's ten writable fields rendered as a live
      input on a row whose committed status is `Closed` - a text box for
      Subject, a select for Status, a select for Priority, a textarea for
      Description, reference pickers for Assigned Group and Assigned
      Agent, and so on - so the only thing standing between an operator
      and an edit to a terminal record was the before-update Business
      Rule ../business_rules/x_casemgmt_block_terminal_closed.xml
      aborting the save after the fact.

      AAP Section 0.5.5's transition matrix, row 8, makes Closed a
      terminal state whose failure surface is the verbatim message
      "Closed cases are terminal and cannot be modified.", and AAP
      Section 0.7.1 requires blocking errors to be surfaced on the form.
      A form that invites an edit and then refuses it satisfies the
      letter of the guard and contradicts the state machine it is
      guarding: the screen tells the operator the record is editable and
      the server tells them it is not.

      This policy makes the form agree with the state machine BEFORE the
      gesture, while the guard continues to be the thing that actually
      enforces it.

    THIS IS PRESENTATION ONLY - THE ENFORCEMENT DOES NOT MOVE

      Nothing here weakens, replaces or duplicates a server-side guard.
      ../business_rules/x_casemgmt_block_terminal_closed.xml (order 100)
      remains the enforcement layer for the terminal state, and
      ../business_rules/x_casemgmt_block_draft_backtransition.xml remains
      the enforcement layer for the Draft prohibition; both are untouched
      by this file and both keep surfacing their verbatim messages.

      That division matters because a UI Policy runs in the browser and
      therefore cannot enforce anything: it does not apply to a write
      through the Table API, to a write from a script, to a list inline
      edit, or to any caller that never renders the form. Every one of
      those paths is still refused by the Business Rule, which compares
      the case's own columns and aborts the write. What this policy adds
      is the one thing the Business Rule cannot do - stop the form from
      inviting the edit in the first place.

      A useful consequence, measured against the guard's own contract:
      because all ten writable controls are read-only, the form can no
      longer produce a dirty field on a Closed case, so pressing Update
      on a Closed case is the "genuine no-op save" that the guard
      explicitly still allows (it excludes `sys_mod_count`,
      `sys_updated_on` and the virtual `duration_to_close` from its
      comparison). The operator therefore sees no error banner for a
      gesture that changed nothing, and still sees the verbatim message
      on any path that does attempt a change.

    WHY DECLARATIVE, AND WHY NOT A CLIENT SCRIPT

      AAP Section 0.7.2 constrains authoring to the platform's
      declarative low-code mechanisms, and the sibling policy file
      establishes the pattern for this application: a `sys_ui_policy`
      with `run_scripts=false`, empty script bodies and one
      `sys_ui_policy_action` per field. An `onLoad` client script calling
      `g_form.setReadOnly()` in a loop would put field-state logic into
      an imperative body that no longer appears in the platform's own
      "UI Policies" surface, would have to be kept in step with every
      future field addition by hand, and would duplicate a capability the
      platform already models as data. There is no imperative logic in
      this file at all.

    WHICH FIELDS GET AN ACTION, AND WHY EXACTLY THESE TEN

      Measured on the verification instance rather than assumed:

        GET /api/now/table/sys_dictionary
            ?sysparm_query=name=x_casemgmt_case^elementISNOTEMPTY
            &sysparm_fields=element,internal_type,read_only,mandatory

      returned `read_only=false` for exactly ten application columns -

          type, status, priority, subject, description,
          requester_name, requester_email, assigned_group,
          assigned_agent, pending_reason

      - and `read_only=true` for the other four the form renders:

          number             AAP Section 0.5.7 "Read-only, format
                             CASE0000001"
          opened_date        auto-set on insert by
                             ../business_rules/x_casemgmt_set_opened_date.xml
          closed_date        auto-set on the Resolved -> Closed
                             transition by
                             ../business_rules/x_casemgmt_set_closed_date.xml
          duration_to_close  a derived glide_duration, unwritable by
                             definition

      The QA screenshot of CASE9000010 corroborates the dictionary
      exactly: those four render as grey static text on the Closed form
      while the other ten render as live controls. So the ten actions
      below are the complete set of fields that a Closed case can
      currently offer for editing, and an eleventh action would be a
      record that changes nothing. This is the AAP Section 0.7.2
      minimal-change clause applied literally: one action per field that
      is actually editable, none for a field the dictionary already
      froze.

      The `sys_*` audit columns (sys_created_by, sys_created_on,
      sys_updated_by, sys_updated_on, sys_mod_count, sys_id) get no
      action either. They are platform-managed columns, they are not on
      the form section
      (../form_layout/sys_ui_section_x_casemgmt_case_default.xml lists
      fourteen elements, none of them a `sys_*` column), and AAP Section
      0.3.2 keeps the application out of out-of-the-box platform
      machinery.

    THE ACTION SHAPE, COLUMN BY COLUMN

      `sys_ui_policy_action` models three independent field states, and
      the column names do not match their labels - verified against
      `sys_dictionary` for the action table and its `sys_choice` rows:

        disabled   label "Read only"; choices ignore | true | false;
                   default ignore.  THIS is the read-only flag.
        mandatory  choices ignore | true | false; default ignore.
        visible    choices ignore | true | false; default ignore.

      Each action below therefore carries:

        disabled  = true     the field is presented read-only on a
                             Closed case. This is the whole point of the
                             record.
        mandatory = ignore   the dictionary's mandatory state is left
                             exactly as it is. Four of these fields
                             (subject, status, description,
                             requester_name) carry mandatory=true in
                             ../dictionary/, and this policy neither
                             adds nor removes a mandatory flag - setting
                             mandatory=false would weaken a constraint
                             this finding never asked to change, and
                             setting mandatory=true would be redundant.
                             Read-only plus mandatory is safe here
                             because a Closed row necessarily has values
                             in all four (both Closed demo cases were
                             checked: subject, status, description and
                             requester_name are populated on each).
        visible   = ignore   nothing is hidden. A Closed case must stay
                             fully readable - it is the audit record of
                             the closed matter, it feeds the dashboards
                             in ../dashboards/ and the reports in
                             ../reports/, and AAP Section 0.5.6 gives
                             `x_casemgmt_case_viewer` read access to all
                             cases. Hiding fields would remove
                             information rather than protect it.
        cleared      = false and
        value_action = ignore  no field value is read, written or
                             cleared by this policy. It cannot alter data.
        field_message / field_message_type  empty, matching the live
                             party action rows (whose field_message_type
                             is the empty string rather than the
                             dictionary default "none"). A per-field
                             message was considered and rejected: the
                             only text that could go there is the
                             verbatim AAP Section 0.7.4 blocking message,
                             and stamping it under ten fields would put
                             that reserved string on screen ten times
                             over on a form where no save has been
                             refused. The read-only rendering is the
                             signal; the verbatim message stays the
                             property of the save that is actually
                             refused.

    reverse_if_false IS FALSE - A DELIBERATE DEVIATION, AND WHY

      The sibling party policy sets `reverse_if_false=true` because its
      job is genuinely two-sided: `party_type` legitimately changes on
      screen, and each state must be undone when the discriminator moves.
      This policy is one-sided, and making it two-sided would introduce a
      second defect in the opposite direction.

      The reverse of `disabled=true` is `disabled=false` - an explicit
      "make this field writable" applied to all ten fields on every
      NON-Closed case form. Two of those fields are the ones AAP Section
      0.5.6 singles out for field-level ACLs: `assigned_group` (write
      restricted to `x_casemgmt_case_manager`) and `assigned_agent`
      (manager, or the assigned agent). A read-only state that a
      field-level ACL or the dictionary put on a control is not this
      application's to reverse, and a policy that announces "writable"
      to an `x_casemgmt_case_viewer` on five of the six statuses would be
      the same "the form invites an edit the server then refuses"
      defect this file exists to close, only pointed at the ACL matrix in
      ../docs/acl-matrix.md instead of at the terminal state.

      Nothing is lost by leaving the reverse off, because there is no
      legitimate in-form path OUT of Closed that would need undoing:

        - A Closed row can never leave Closed. The status move is
          refused by x_casemgmt_block_terminal_closed and the `status`
          control is itself read-only under this policy.
        - On a non-Closed row the condition is simply false and this
          policy contributes nothing, leaving ../dictionary/, the
          field-level ACLs in ../acl/ and the platform as the only
          authorities on field state - which is the correct answer.
        - The one remaining path is an operator on a Resolved case
          choosing "Closed" from the status list rather than pressing the
          Close button. The policy re-evaluates on that change (the
          platform takes `status` as a watched field from the condition
          itself, independently of reverse_if_false) and freezes the
          form. That freeze is not reversible on screen under EITHER
          setting, because `status` is one of the fields it freezes, so
          reverse_if_false=true would buy nothing there; a reload
          restores the true state, and the supported path into Closed
          remains the ../ui_action/x_casemgmt_case_close.xml button,
          which is a server-side action (`client=false`) and never
          rewrites the form's status control.

      `on_load=true` is what makes the policy do its job on the surface
      the finding is about: opening an already-Closed case. `global=true`
      applies it to every view of the form rather than one named view.
      `inherit=false` because no table extends x_casemgmt_case.
      `run_scripts=false` with both script bodies empty - there is no
      imperative logic to maintain and no dead script is carried.

    WHAT THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO

      - It does not remove or disable the form's `Update` and `Delete`
        buttons. Those are out-of-the-box `sys_ui_action` records in the
        GLOBAL scope; AAP Section 0.3.2 prohibits global-scope changes of
        any kind and AAP Section 0.7.2 restates it as scoped-namespace
        exclusivity, so they are out of bounds for this package by rule.
        With all ten controls read-only, `Update` can only produce the
        no-op save the terminal guard already allows, and `Delete`
        remains governed by the delete ACLs in ../acl/ (manager only),
        exactly as AAP Section 0.5.6 specifies. The residual `Update`
        button is recorded as a named open limitation in
        ../docs/state-machine.md.
      - It does not touch the six transition buttons in ../ui_action/.
        They need no help: each one's visibility condition is evaluated
        server-side against `current` and requires an exact non-Closed
        source status (Draft, Open, In Progress, Pending or Resolved), so
        none of them renders on a Closed case. That was re-verified
        against all six records and against the QA screenshot of
        CASE9000010, whose only buttons are the two global ones.
      - It does not affect list inline editing, which the platform
        governs through ACLs rather than UI Policies, and which the
        terminal guard refuses server-side.

    XML CONVENTIONS HONORED (matching the sibling policy file)
      - Wrapper element: `<unload unload_date="2025-01-01 00:00:00">`,
        the dominant payload convention in this package.
      - Element ordering: alphabetical within each record.
      - Only columns that actually exist on the target tables are
        emitted. `sys_ui_policy` has no `name` column (its `sys_name` is
        derived from `short_description`), so no `<name>` element is
        written; `sys_ui_policy_action` has no `sys_domain` or
        `sys_overrides` column, so neither is written on the action rows.
      - `<sys_scope>` carries the scoped application's sys_id with a
        `display_value` attribute and `<sys_package>` carries the scope
        source, which is the exact shape the already-deployed sibling
        policy uses and which was confirmed to resolve on this instance
        (a live read of both party rows returns sys_scope = sys_package =
        82b99028936f74320d74d6f88357a5af).
      - `<sys_update_name>` is stated explicitly on all eleven records.
        Both tables extend Application File, and the platform derives the
        key as `sys_ui_policy_<sys_id>` / `sys_ui_policy_action_<sys_id>`
        - verified by reading the live party rows, whose sys_update_name
        values are exactly that. Stating it makes the key the import
        engine matches on visible in the artifact instead of implicit in
        the pinned sys_id, so a re-import UPDATES these rows rather than
        adding a second policy.
      - `<ui_policy>` on each action carries its parent policy's sys_id
        with a `display_value` attribute. The parent is defined in THIS
        file and is carried in the same Update Set, and the preview
        resolver matches an intra-set reference through the target
        block's canonical `<table>_<sys_id>` update name, so the
        reference previews clean on a fresh instance.
      - `<order>100</order>`: this is the only UI Policy on
        x_casemgmt_case (a live query of sys_ui_policy for
        `tableSTARTSWITHx_casemgmt` returns only the two party rows), so
        no other policy can contend for these fields and the order value
        is uncontested. 100 matches the application's 100-step ordering
        convention.
      - `<ui_type>0</ui_type>` is "Desktop", per the `sys_choice` rows for
        `sys_ui_policy.ui_type` (0 Desktop, 1 Mobile / Service Portal, 10
        All). It matches the sibling party policy and the platform's own
        norm (2,612 of the instance's UI Policies are 0), and it is the
        right value here because the surface this finding is about is the
        internal UI16 case form. The public pages in ../portal/ never
        render this form - they are widget-driven - so nothing is lost by
        not selecting "All".
      - No PII: these records carry structural state only.

    IDENTIFIERS
      Every sys_id is a recomputable MD5 of a canonical string, the same
      derivation the sibling artifacts in this package use:

        policy   md5("x_casemgmt_case.ui_policy.closed_readonly")
                 e13c3e9e50df1b846271700b571c5a82
        actions  md5("x_casemgmt_case.ui_policy_action.closed_readonly."
                     + the action's field name)
                 c70139ca5b3e4ce81311b2f2fbda2538  subject
                 4805b2840552271409e1682f77845510  type
                 d27870f5faf1756f9e093cdcc241e14f  status
                 f85a55a85fdd166fced53756f266093e  priority
                 3f31af883ca60ee992339571ad02274a  description
                 e54e957feb4e512c65a2baf64a3f819a  requester_name
                 2ac5b372ace69b0d2e9c9d2fecbc708f  requester_email
                 a4ced0d7fbd97388a4338fa56ad313f4  assigned_group
                 1859fc68a1e6d7e05bfdbf8753de5336  assigned_agent
