      Recomputable via, e.g.
        printf '%s' 'x_casemgmt_case.ui_policy_action.closed_readonly.status' \
            | md5sum | cut -c1-32
      so regenerating this file is byte-deterministic. All eleven values
      were checked against `sys_metadata` on the verification instance
      and against every file in this package: none is in use, so these
      records cannot collide with an existing row.

      These are the records' OWN primary keys. No reference field in this
      file points at a foreign record by sys_id except `<sys_scope>`,
      which follows the sibling policy's convention and carries the
      scope's `display_value`; every table, field and role named
      anywhere in this file is named by NAME, per AAP Section 0.7.2's
      no-hardcoded-sys_id constraint. There is no script body, so there
      is no sys_id literal in any condition or script.

    CROSS-REFERENCES (AAP Section 0.5.2 dependency ordering)
      THESE RECORDS MUST LOAD AFTER:
        - ../app/sys_app/x_casemgmt_case_management.xml (the scoped
          application record, against which <sys_scope>/<sys_package>
          resolve)
        - ../tables/x_casemgmt_case.xml (parent table; the policy and all
          ten actions target this table by name)
        - ../dictionary/x_casemgmt_case_status.xml (the field named by the
          condition) and the nine other
          ../dictionary/x_casemgmt_case_*.xml entries for the fields the
          actions operate on - an action whose field does not yet exist
          is dropped on import
        - ../choices/sys_choice_case_status.xml (supplies the 'Closed'
          condition literal)
      Within this file the `sys_ui_policy` MUST load before the ten
      `sys_ui_policy_action` rows that reference it; the records are
      written in that order.

      THESE RECORDS MUST LOAD BEFORE:
        - ../seed-data/cases/x_casemgmt_case_demo_*.xml (so the two
          Closed demo cases, CASE9000006 and CASE9000010, open read-only
          the first time a verifier opens them post-commit)

      RELATED:
        - ../business_rules/x_casemgmt_block_terminal_closed.xml - the
          enforcement layer this policy presents. Unchanged.
        - ../form_layout/sys_ui_section_x_casemgmt_case_default.xml - the
          single-column section whose fourteen elements this policy's ten
          actions cover the writable subset of.
        - ../docs/state-machine.md - the Closed-state narrative, which
          now documents this policy and the limitations that remain
          around it.

    CONDITION STRING
      status=Closed
      A single-term encoded query against the state field. The
      right-hand literal MUST match the `<value>` of the corresponding
      record in ../choices/sys_choice_case_status.xml byte-for-byte; it
      was read back from the live sys_choice rows for
      x_casemgmt_case.status, which are Draft, Open, In Progress,
      Pending, Resolved and Closed, in that sequence.

    AAP COMPLIANCE SUMMARY
      - AAP 0.5.5 row 8 (Closed is terminal, "Closed cases are terminal
        and cannot be modified."): the form now presents the terminal
        state instead of contradicting it.
      - AAP 0.7.1 (surface blocking errors on the form): unchanged and
        reinforced - the verbatim message is still written by the
        Business Rule on any attempted change, and the form no longer
        solicits the change that would produce it.
      - AAP 0.7.2 (declarative low-code authoring): a UI Policy with
        declarative actions, `run_scripts=false`, no script body.
      - AAP 0.7.2 (scoped-namespace exclusivity): `<sys_scope>`,
        `<sys_package>` and `<table>` all reference the scoped
        application and its `x_casemgmt_case` table.
      - AAP 0.7.2 (minimal change): one policy, one behaviour, one
        action per editable field; no field added, removed, relabelled,
        retyped or reordered, and no existing record modified.
      - AAP 0.7.2 (no hard-coded sys_id): every table, field and choice
        literal is named by name; the only sys_id values are these
        records' own keys and the scope reference the sibling policy
        already uses.
      - AAP 0.3.2 (no global-scope write): the global `Update` and
        `Delete` UI Actions and the platform's field macros are left
        exactly as the platform renders them.
      - AAP 0.5.6 / ../docs/acl-matrix.md: nothing here grants or
        withdraws access; `mandatory` and `visible` are both left alone
        and the reverse is off, so no field-level ACL state is
        overridden on any status.
      - Single Update Set deliverable (AAP 0.7.2): these eleven records
        are carried as eleven blocks of the exported Update Set.
-->
